Description: Wild OS restore points and start-up recovery
 Restore points before every update and driver install, going back to one
 from Settings or at start-up, start-up recovery after two failed starts or
 with Shift held, and Reset Wild OS from a signed copy of the release kept
 on the PC.
 .
 On a Btrfs system a restore point is a read-only snapshot of the system
 subvolume; on ext4 it is a file copy hard-linked to the one before. Each
 keeps its own kernel and start-up files, so going back never meets a
 kernel from a newer system. Personal files, Wi-Fi, accounts and Windows
 mode stay as they are.
 .
 Start-up recovery runs from the reset copy inside the encrypted disk,
 independent of the installed system. Everything stays on the PC; nothing
 is sent anywhere.
